1. Business Background
RetailIQ is an AI solution for the Retail and E-Commerce industry. The project is based on an omnichannel retail business that sells through different sales channels. The business has a large amount of transaction data that includes information about customers, products, sales channels, regions, and dates. This information can be used to understand customer spending and support better business decisions.
The retail business needs to make better use of the historical data that it already collects. Different customers may spend different amounts depending on their customer information, the product category, the sales channel, the region, and the time of the transaction. Looking at these factors together can help the business understand the patterns in its sales.
RetailIQ uses machine learning to turn the available transaction data into an estimate of the expected transaction value. Instead of only looking at past sales manually, the solution learns from historical records and uses the information in the dataset to estimate sales_amount. This allows the retail business to use its existing data in a more useful and data-driven way.
The project is therefore focused on using Artificial Intelligence and Python to solve business problems in Retail and E-Commerce. The solution is designed to provide a simple prediction that can be understood by users without requiring them to perform the calculations manually.
2. Problem Statement
Retail businesses need to understand how much customers are likely to spend in a transaction. Customer spending can be different depending on customer information, product category, sales channel, region, and time. If a business only uses manual calculations or simple averages, it may not get a clear view of customer spending.
The problem is therefore the lack of a simple, data-driven method for estimating transaction value from historical retail data. A large amount of transaction information may be available, but the business still needs a way to use that information to identify patterns and estimate the expected sales amount.
This problem affects the way the business understands its customers and sales. Without a machine learning solution, users may have to depend on manual calculations and basic summaries when trying to understand customer spending. This can make it more difficult to compare actual sales with expected sales and to use the available data effectively.
3. Solution
RetailIQ is an AI-based model designed to estimate the final transaction value (sales amount) at checkout for a customer. The model is trained and tested using historical retail data captured from 2024–2025. The solution uses quantity, unit_price, discount_pct, date, sales_channel, payment method, and region to make the prediction. The solution also considers data leakage by avoiding variables that directly calculate sales_amount, like using engineered features such as gross_sales and discount_amount. This allows the model to learn useful patterns instead of being given the exact mathematical formula for the answer.
4. How will it benefit the community?
The proposed AI solution can benefit surrounding communities by helping local retail and e-commerce businesses make better-informed sales decisions. The model predicts the expected sales amount of a transaction using factors such as quantity, unit price, discount, date, sales channel, payment method, and region. This information can help businesses better understand expected transaction values and make more informed decisions about their sales activities. Stronger and more sustainable local businesses can benefit surrounding communities by continuing to provide goods and services, attracting and retaining customers, and contributing to local economic activity. As local businesses and the economy become more sustainable, increased business activity and competition may also contribute to better access to goods and more affordable prices for consumers. This can help reduce financial hardship and poverty levels within surrounding communities. Therefore, although the model does not directly provide a community service, its use by local retailers can support more informed business decision-making and contribute indirectly to the economic sustainability and well-being of the surrounding community.
